The linker error indicates that the device driver instances for the DAC emulators are not being created. This typically happens when the Zephyr build system fails to correctly identify the device tree nodes as instances of a particular driver.

The device tree overlay `native_sim.overlay` uses non-standard node names (`dac-emulator-0`, `dac-emulator-1`, `dac-emulator-many`) for multiple instances of the same device. While technically valid device tree syntax, this can confuse Zephyr's scripts that generate driver instance information.

The standard convention in Zephyr is to use the `name@address` format for multiple instances of a device. By renaming the nodes to `dac-emulator@0`, `dac-emulator@1`, and `dac-emulator@2`, we align with this convention. This allows the build system to correctly identify all three nodes as instances of the `zephyr,dac-emulator` compatible, ensuring that the driver's instantiation macros are expanded correctly and the device objects are created and linked.

The C code uses device tree labels (`dac_emul0`, etc.) to get device pointers, so it is not affected by this change in node names.

